Chronic Spontaneous Urticaria
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Pruritic erythematous wheals >6 weeks, Transitory lesions resolving <24 hours, Absence of recognizable external triggers, Normal basic inflammatory markers

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-009
Reason for Visit: Widespread, intensely pruritic rash causing severe distress, sleep deprivation, and high anxiety regarding potential anaphylaxis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed a distressed patient with a rapid-onset, widespread, intensely itchy erythematous rash that has worsened over the evening. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively assess airway patency, definitively rule out impending anaphylaxis or severe angioedema, and provide immediate pharmacological relief for the intolerable pruritus before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I am covered in these intensely itchy red welts, I haven't slept in days, and I'm terrified my throat is going to close up."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with a recurrent, almost daily appearance of intensely pruritic erythematous wheals, a pattern persisting for 8 weeks. The lesions have a central raised pallor surrounded by a prominent red flare. The lesions are migratory; individual welts resolve completely without a trace within 24 hours. The intense itching strictly exacerbates at night, leading to severe sleep disruption and generalized daytime fatigue. The patient reports a complete absence of recognizable external triggers (foods, new medications, soaps). The patient firmly denies difficulty breathing, wheeze, or lip/tongue swelling.
Functional Status: Independent but severely fatigued and acutely agitated due to the pruritus.
Social/Safety Concerns: Profound sleep deprivation impacting daily functioning and mood.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/78 | HR: 85 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Visibly uncomfortable, constantly scratching extremities and torso, appearing exhausted.
Physical Exam: Widespread erythematous wheals with central pallor noted across the trunk and proximal limbs. The intensely red lesions exhibit complete blanching upon applied firm physical pressure. No dermatographism noted. Complete absence of excoriation-induced secondary bacterial infection. Airway is completely patent; no stridor, hoarseness, or wheeze. Complete absence of deep tissue angioedema (lips, eyelids, tongue are normal). Documented history of normal baseline full blood count and normal inflammatory markers (ESR/CRP).
Environmental Observations: Bedroom is overly warm; heavy blankets on the bed which may exacerbate nocturnal vasodilation and pruritus.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking over-the-counter loratadine 10mg daily with minimal to zero effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Chronic Spontaneous Urticaria - Status: Severe acute flare
Differentials:

Anaphylaxis/Acute Allergic Reaction - Ruled out due to normal hemodynamics, complete absence of airway compromise, and chronic 8-week fluctuating history.

Urticarial Vasculitis - Ruled out clinically as lesions resolve completely within 24 hours without leaving residual purpura or bruising.

Scabies - Ruled out due to the transient nature of individual wheals and absence of typical burrows in web spaces.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours assessment due to a severe, highly distressing flare of widespread urticaria associated with intense pruritus and high patient anxiety regarding potential anaphylaxis. The severity of the symptoms and the patient's panic necessitated an immediate, hands-on physical examination to definitively verify airway patency, exclude systemic anaphylaxis, and rule out angioedema. Confirming the condition as a benign, though highly irritating, spontaneous flare allowed for immediate pharmacological up-titration of antihistamines and prevented an inappropriate, high-stress presentation to the emergency department, fulfilling the criteria for MBS Item 597.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Promethazine 25mg stat for immediate nocturnal sedation and H1 blockade. Advised to up-titrate non-sedating antihistamines (e.g., Cetirizine up to 4x standard dose daily) strictly under GP supervision.
Positive Behavioral Change: Instead of scratching the wheals, aggressively apply a cold, damp facecloth to the affected area for 2 minutes. The intense cold rapidly overrides the itch nerve signals (gate control theory), rewarding the behavioral shift with immediate, non-destructive relief and preventing skin damage.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 3-5 days to formalize a high-dose antihistamine regimen or refer to immunology/dermatology.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation or call 000 if developing any swelling of the lips, tongue, or throat, difficulty breathing, wheezing, or a feeling of impending doom.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis relies on the absence of systemic symptoms; new onset of joint pain or fevers requires prompt medical review.
Clinical Course: This is a misfiring of your immune system's mast cells, not a true allergy to a specific trigger. It can persist for months to years but is generally self-limiting and manageable with high-dose antihistamines.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call an ambulance immediately if you experience any swelling in your mouth or throat, a change in your voice, or any difficulty breathing.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune cells are releasing histamine spontaneously. A standard single dose of antihistamine is often completely insufficient; the receptors need to be continuously blocked with higher, regular doses to prevent the wheals from forming.
Actionable Steps: Keep the bedroom actively cool and use light cotton bedding. Reducing the skin temperature directly decreases blood flow to the superficial skin, physically reducing the size and itch of the wheals, thus improving your ability to sleep.

